{
  "text": "Imagine you're running a lemonade stand. \n\n**CAC (Customer Acquisition Cost)** is like figuring out how much money you spent to get one customer to buy your lemonade. This includes things like the cost of your lemons, sugar, water, the sign you made, and even the time you spent shouting about your lemonade. If you spent $10 on ingredients and marketing, and you got 5 customers, your CAC would be $2 per customer ($10 / 5 customers).\n\n**LTV (Lifetime Value)** is how much profit you expect to make from that one customer over the entire time they buy from you. If a customer buys lemonade from you every week for a month, and each time you make $1 profit, their LTV would be $4 ($1 profit/week * 4 weeks).\n\n**Why are they important together?** You want your LTV to be much higher than your CAC. If you spend $2 to get a customer (CAC) but they only bring you $1 in profit (LTV), you're losing money! But if you spend $2 to get a customer and they bring you $10 in profit, that's a great business. It helps you understand if your business model is sustainable and profitable.",
